<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Hipponion</h1><p class="st-meta">650 BCE – 192 BCE (approx.) · capital Hipponion</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Colony of Locri on the Tyrrhenian coast of Calabria, known for an Orphic gold tablet, which became the Roman colony of Vibo Valentia in 192 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/locri-epizephyrii">Locri Epizephyrii</a> founded Hipponion in the 7th century BCE on a hill above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-sant-eufemia">Gulf of Sant'Eufemia</a>, giving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locrians">Locrians</a> a port on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrrhenian-sea">Tyrrhenian Sea</a> and a share in the trade crossing the Calabrian isthmus.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Hipponion depended on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/locri-epizephyrii">Locri</a> for much of its history and shared its alliances. It had temples, a city wall and a port. A gold leaf found in a woman's grave of about 400 BCE gives Orphic instructions for the soul in the underworld and is a key text for Greek mystery religion.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysius-i-of-syracuse">Dionysius I</a> destroyed Hipponion in 389 BCE and moved its people to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syracuse">Syracuse</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> restored them in 379 BCE. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bruttii">Bruttii</a> captured it around 356 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agathocles-of-syracuse">Agathocles</a> briefly held it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vibo-valentia">Vibo Valentia</a> there in 192 BCE, today's Vibo Valentia.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hipponion.jpg" alt="Hipponion" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: DomBeats93 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Veduta_aerea_del_castello_di_Vibo_Valentia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>650 BCE – 192 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hipponion</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Vibo Valentia (ancient), Hipponium</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-421&amp;focus=hipponion" aria-label="Hipponion on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5080 790 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hipponion lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5400 1046L5405 1060M5400 1046L5402 1025M5400 1046L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5405 1060h0M5402 1025h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5400 1046h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5400 1046h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-421&amp;focus=hipponion">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
